  <h2>Which one should you pick</h2>

  <p><strong>Fluently</strong> is the better tool if you already hold conversations in English and want to sound sharper in meetings and interviews, you're on Android, or your employer is paying through its teams plan.</p>

  <p><strong>Vocele</strong> is the better tool if you understand far more than you can say, you want to see a specific sound improve in numbers, you want words to come back until they stick, or the conversation you're dreading is with a doctor, a landlord or your kid's teacher rather than a client.</p>

  <p>If you want a big guided lesson library at a low price, look at <a href="/vs/praktika">Vocele vs Praktika</a>; for 130+ languages and a free daily allowance, <a href="/vs/talkpal">Vocele vs TalkPal</a>. For a pronunciation-first English app, see <a href="/vs/elsa">Vocele vs ELSA Speak</a>; for a polished beginner course, <a href="/vs/speak">Vocele vs Speak</a>. All seven are sorted by problem in <a href="/blog/best-ai-language-tutor-app">the best AI language tutor app in 2026</a>. And if you're weighing any app against a human tutor at $200 to $320 a month, <a href="/blog/ai-speaking-coach-vs-human-tutor">the AI coach vs human tutor comparison</a> is honest about where the apps lose.</p>

  <p>Vocele isn't on the App Store yet. If the per-word scoring or the corrections in your own sentence are what you came for, <a href="/pricing">see the plans and the 3-day trial</a>; I'll update this page when the listing goes live.</p>
